<h2>One household, two applications</h2>
<p>Nothing in the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 restricts a household to a single application. The Act protects <em>any woman</em> in a domestic relationship, and a joint family contains women on both sides of every dispute. A common pattern in Delhi courts: the daughter-in-law files an application under Section 12 against the husband and his parents; within weeks, the mother-in-law — herself a woman living in the same shared household — files her own Section 12 application describing the daughter-in-law’s conduct as verbal, emotional or economic abuse. Each side then finds itself simultaneously complainant in one proceeding and respondent in the other.</p>
<p>The phenomenon is not an abuse of the statute in itself. Domestic violence within a joint family can genuinely run in more than one direction, and the Act’s definitions do not sort aggressors and victims by generation. What the rival filings demand is careful attention to two definitions, and disciplined case management thereafter.</p>

<h2>Reading Sections 2(a) and 2(q) in both directions</h2>
<p>Section 2(a) defines the aggrieved person as any woman who is, or has been, in a domestic relationship with the respondent and who alleges domestic violence. Age and generational position are irrelevant: a mother-in-law and a daughter-in-law living together are family members in a joint family, and therefore stand in a domestic relationship with each other under Section 2(f). On the complainant’s side, the definition accommodates both women equally.</p>
<p>The asymmetry appears on the respondent’s side. Section 2(q) as enacted defines the respondent as an adult male person in a domestic relationship with the aggrieved person, and its proviso then permits an <em>aggrieved wife or female living in a relationship in the nature of marriage</em> to complain also against a relative of the husband or male partner. The daughter-in-law’s application against her mother-in-law travels comfortably through that proviso. The mother-in-law’s application against her daughter-in-law does not, because she is not complaining against a relative of her own husband. In practice, proceedings against female family members are entertained where the domestic relationship and specific allegations against them are established, but a drafter acting for the elder generation should anticipate the definitional objection — for instance by arraying the son where his conduct is genuinely part of the grievance, and by considering whether the Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act, 2007 is the surer statutory route for the relief actually wanted.</p>

<h2>How rival Section 12 applications are managed</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><h4>1. Two filings, two reports</h4><p>Each application is registered separately, and the Protection Officer may prepare a domestic incident report in each. Neither filing extinguishes or stays the other.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><h4>2. Listing before one court</h4><p>The later application concerning the same household is ordinarily placed before, or transferred to, the court seized of the earlier one, so that one judicial mind supervises both. Each party should disclose the rival proceeding at the first hearing.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><h4>3. Joint hearing, common evidence</h4><p>Section 28(1) applies the ordinary criminal procedure, and Section 28(2) allows the court to lay down its own procedure for applications under Section 12 or Section 23(2). Courts use this flexibility to hear the applications together and, with consent or by direction, to record evidence commonly, with cross-examination open to both sides in both matters.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><h4>4. Separate adjudication</h4><p>Consolidated hearing does not mean a single verdict. Each application is decided on its own pleadings and proof, and the reliefs — protection, residence, monetary or compensation orders — are tailored to each aggrieved person independently.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The interim stage needs the greatest care. Section 23 permits ex parte interim orders, and rival applications about the same premises can generate contradictory directions — one order protecting the daughter-in-law’s residence, another restraining her from entering portions of the same house. Once both matters sit before one court, the Magistrate can mould interim arrangements that respect both applications until evidence is complete, and either side may seek alteration under Section 25 if circumstances change.</p>

<h2>The Senior Citizens Act dimension</h2>
<p>Cross-litigation in a joint household frequently straddles two statutes. The elder generation may invoke the Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act, 2007, which creates a Maintenance Tribunal for claims against children and relatives, declares in Section 3 that its provisions override inconsistent laws, and in Delhi is supplemented by rules providing machinery for protecting the life and property of senior citizens. The daughter-in-law’s DV application and the parents’ Tribunal proceeding can run in parallel: Section 36 of the DV Act states that the Act is in addition to, and not in derogation of, any other law. Neither proceeding abates the other, but every order obtained in one forum should be placed on record in the other, since each adjudicator must know what arrangements already govern the same house and the same family.</p>

<h2>Drafting and responding to a cross-application</h2>
<div class="check"><ul>
<li>Plead date-wise, incident-specific allegations against each respondent; omnibus accusations against the entire family invite objection in either direction.</li>
<li>Disclose the rival application, its number and its stage in the first pleading filed after it comes to knowledge.</li>
<li>Resist mirror-image drafting: an application that merely inverts the other side’s allegations reads as retaliation and is tested as such in cross-examination.</li>
<li>Seek listing of both matters before the same court, and a common schedule for evidence where appropriate.</li>
<li>Preserve contemporaneous material — medical papers, police complaints, society or landlord correspondence — since rival narratives are usually resolved on documents, not assertions.</li>
<li>When responding, meet each incident with particulars; pleading the timing of the cross-application as retaliatory is legitimate, but it supplements, and never replaces, an incident-wise reply.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="note"><p>Rival applications are decided on evidence, not on who reached the court first. A genuinely aggrieved woman on either side of the generational divide loses nothing because the other side has also filed — and gains nothing by filing a retaliatory copy. How these principles apply to a particular household is fact-specific, and independent legal advice on specific facts is always advisable.</p></div>
